      <h2>Case Study: Multi-Platform Benefit Launch — Pharmacy Rewards</h2>

      <h3>The Problem</h3>
      <p>The existing Pharmacy Rewards benefit of ExtraCare was highly confusing to customers and had a difficult opt-in process. The team decided to test a new benefit and launch new ways to engage with the benefit, to determine if the program was worth scaling. Testing a new, highly anticipated incentive benefit required rolling it out seamlessly across an incredibly fragmented ecosystem, spanning four distinct software platforms simultaneously.</p>

      <h3>The UX Intervention</h3>
      <p>I designed a modular, omni-channel reward component framework. I reworked the enrollment, opt in, and "add a member" flows. We created celebration moments to drive sales with the earned rewards, and we standardized the reward tracking visualization. I paired directly with engineering teams to ensure that tokenized reward states were consistently visualized, instantly updated, and legally compliant across all four platforms.</p>

      <h3>The Rollout & Results</h3>
      <p>We launched a highly targeted pilot program across 6 states. The user experience was frictionless, and user adoption instantaneous. So much so, that the program was too successful, driving unprecedented engagement volumes that required us to strategically recalibrate and scale back the reward caps to balance financial modeling.</p>

      <!-- Reward component framework & celebration -->
      <img src="/images/lnp/live_18.png" alt="Reward component framework across platforms" class="project-image" loading="lazy" />
      <img src="/images/lnp/live_19.png" alt="Reward tracking visualization" class="project-image" loading="lazy" />
      <img src="/images/lnp/live_20.png" alt="Celebration moment UI" class="project-image" loading="lazy" />

      <p>The process started with a use case analysis of profiles, accounts and cards. Our loyalty ecosystem is complex and it confuses customers. We were looking for a way to simplify the process and communicate clearly to the customer how different accounts and benefits would be connected. Eventually, these different use cases became different flows in the hand-off document to engineering.</p>

      <p>The next step was analyzing the current experience, to determine what needed to be updated. What the team found, was the following:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The experience is mostly colleague facing, leaving no agency for the customers to make decisions</li>
        <li>Colleagues are not aware of how different accounts link to each other, and often make mistakes throughout the process.</li>
        <li>There isn't a confirmation and celebration moment that can communicate to the customer that their reward has been issued.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Analysis Findings -->
      <div class="image-grid-3">
        <img src="/images/lnp/live_21.png" alt="Current experience pain point 1" loading="lazy" />
        <img src="/images/lnp/live_22.png" alt="Current experience pain point 2" loading="lazy" />
